(there are two parties: the prover and the verifier. The prover asserts that it has a solution) Tj
T*
(to a \(generally NP-complete\) problem. The prover can convince the verifier of this without) Tj
T*
(sharing the actual solution to the problem. The canonical example is 3-coloring a graph. That) Tj
T*
(is, the prover asserts that, for a given \(shared\) graph, it has a valid 3-coloring. It wants to) Tj
T*
(convince the verifier of this without revealing the actual color assignment. As a quick recap,) Tj
T*
(graph coloring is the problem where given a graph, we find a way to assign each node a color) Tj
T*
(such that no two adjacent nodes have the same color. 3-coloring is coloring with at most 3) Tj
T*
(colors. %0 0 0 1 1 0--1 2 2 0--2 1--2 3 3 2--3 4 4 3--4 4--0 %0 0 0 1 1 0--1 2 2 0--2 1...) Tj
T*
